---
const sections = [
  { id: 'start', label: 'The field guide' },
  { id: 'hardware', label: 'Know your GPU' },
  { id: 'memory', label: 'Move the bytes' },
  { id: 'matrices', label: 'Multiply the numbers' },
  { id: 'reuse', label: 'Reuse the weights' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Find the speed limit' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'Trust the measurements' },
  { id: 'tokens', label: 'One token at a time' },
  { id: 'cache', label: 'Remember the work' },
  { id: 'roofline', label: 'Find the bottleneck' },
  { id: 'precision', label: 'Make room for more' },
  { id: 'batching', label: 'Keep the GPU busy' },
];
const base = '/concepts/llm-inference-v2/learn';
---
<div class="inference-book" data-book>
  <div class="book-toolbar">
    <span class="book-edition">A VISUAL FIELD GUIDE <span>BY RANGARAJ P</span></span>
    <a href="#chapters">Full handbook →</a>
  </div>
  <nav class="book-tabs" aria-label="Visual book contents">
    {sections.map((s, i) => <a href={`#${s.id}`} data-book-tab={i} aria-controls={`spread-${s.id}`}><span>0{i}</span> {s.label}</a>)}
  </nav>
  <div class="book-stage">
    <div class="book-binding" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="book-spreads">
      <section class="book-spread cover-spread" id="spread-start" data-spread="start" aria-labelledby="title-start">
        <div class="book-page book-cover">
          <p class="book-kicker">THE OPEN ENGINEERING NOTEBOOK / VOL. 01</p>
          <h2 id="title-start">Inference<br/>engineering<span>Illustrated.</span></h2>
          <p class="cover-subtitle">Less mystery.<br/>More mental models.</p>
          <div class="cover-orbits" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>t → t + 1</span></div>
          <div class="cover-byline"><span>RANGARAJ P</span><span>IDEAS → IMPLEMENTATIONS</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page opening-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">BEFORE THE EQUATIONS</p>
          <h3>See it move.<br/><em>Then make it click.</em></h3>
          <p>A language model answers one token at a time. Making that process fast is a story about remembering work, moving bytes, and keeping hardware busy.</p>
          <p>This little book makes that story tangible. Turn a page, change a control, and watch one idea at a time.</p>
          <div class="learning-route"><span>01 <b>Watch a request unfold</b></span><span>02 <b>Try a small experiment</b></span><span>03 <b>Follow the deeper chapter</b></span></div>
          <button class="book-button" data-book-go="1">Open the first lesson <span>→</span></button>
          <p class="book-note">Six interactive explanations. No 3D graphics required.<br/>Use the buttons or your left and right arrow keys.</p>
        </div>
      </section>


      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-hardware" data-spread="hardware" aria-labelledby="title-hardware">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / UNDERSTAND THE MACHINE</p>
          <h3 id="title-hardware">A GPU is a factory<br/><em>for numbers.</em></h3>
          <p>Imagine a warehouse, a delivery team, and a room full of workers. The warehouse stores materials. The delivery team moves them. The workers turn them into results.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THINK OF IT THIS WAY</span><p>GPU memory (<strong>VRAM</strong>) is the warehouse. The <strong>SMs</strong> are the workers. <strong>Bandwidth</strong> is how fast materials arrive.</p></div>
          <p>If workers wait for deliveries, the task is <strong>memory-bound</strong>. If deliveries arrive faster than workers can process them, the task is <strong>compute-bound</strong>.</p>
          <p>Neural networks multiply enormous matrices. Reusing the same weights for more inputs can help the workers stay busy. The <strong>roofline</strong> describes where the bottleneck changes.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/gpu-fundamentals/`}>Go deeper: hardware foundations →</a>
          <span class="page-number">02</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment" data-hardware-demo>
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / THE GPU FACTORY</span><span class="live-tag">CHANGE THE SPEED</span></div>
          <p class="demo-caption">Move the sliders. Watch the workers and the queue.</p>
          <div class="hardware-factory" aria-label="GPU factory showing delivery and worker activity">
            <div class="hardware-factory-top"><div class="hardware-station"><strong>VRAM</strong><span>Warehouse</span></div><div class="hardware-track"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="hardware-station workers"><strong>SMs</strong><span>Workers</span></div></div>
            <div class="hardware-queue"><span>WAITING WORK</span><div data-hardware-queue><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><small data-hardware-queue-label>Balanced</small></div>
            <div class="hardware-workers" aria-label="Worker status"><span>WORKER STATUS</span><div data-hardware-workers><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><small data-hardware-worker-label>Working</small></div>
          </div>
          <label class="range-label" for="hardware-delivery">Delivery speed <output id="hardware-delivery-value">2 / 3</output></label>
          <input id="hardware-delivery" type="range" min="1" max="3" step="1" value="2" />
          <label class="range-label" for="hardware-compute">Compute speed <output id="hardware-compute-value">2 / 3</output></label>
          <input id="hardware-compute" type="range" min="1" max="3" step="1" value="2" />
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-hardware-explanation aria-live="polite">Delivery and compute are balanced. The workers have enough data to keep moving.</p>
          <div class="hardware-roofline">
            <div><strong>THE ROOFLINE CONNECTION</strong><span data-hardware-roofline-label>Balanced resources</span></div>
            <div class="hardware-roofline-chart" role="img" aria-label="A sloped memory bandwidth ceiling transitions to a flat compute ceiling">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 400 120" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 10 V100 H388" stroke="#9da995" fill="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M22 100 L215 28 H386" stroke="#214d3c" fill="none" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="215" cy="28" r="5" fill="#b77b48"/><text x="70" y="57" font-size="10" fill="#66776b">MEMORY LIMIT</text><text x="270" y="18" font-size="10" fill="#66776b">COMPUTE LIMIT</text></svg>
            </div>
            <p>Left: workers wait for bytes. Right: workers are busy calculating. The bend is where the limiting resource changes.</p>
          </div>
          <span class="page-number">03</span>
        </div>
      </section>


      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-memory" data-spread="memory" aria-labelledby="title-memory">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · MEMORY</p>
          <h3 id="title-memory">The warehouse is big.<br/><em>The doorway has a speed limit.</em></h3>
          <p>A GPU stores numbers in video memory, or <strong>VRAM</strong>. Its <strong>capacity</strong> tells us how much fits. Its <strong>bandwidth</strong> tells us how quickly data can move. These are different properties.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THINK OF IT THIS WAY</span><p>A warehouse may hold thousands of boxes, but a narrow loading dock still limits how quickly workers receive them.</p></div>
          <p>Copying an array means reading every number from its source and writing every number to its destination. Both directions count as traffic.</p>
          <p><strong>FP16</strong> means each number occupies two bytes. Sixteen million FP16 values occupy 32 MB; copying them moves about 64 MB in total.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/gpu-fundamentals/`}>Read the detailed memory explanation →</a>
          <span class="page-number">04</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FOLLOW THE BYTES</span><span class="live-tag">MEMORY COPY</span></div>
          <p class="demo-caption">Choose how many FP16 values to copy.</p>
          <div class="hardware-factory hardware-copy"><div class="hardware-factory-top"><div class="hardware-station"><strong>Source</strong><span>Read</span></div><div class="hardware-track"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="hardware-station workers"><strong>Destination</strong><span>Write</span></div></div></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="copy-count">Number of values <output id="copy-count-value">16 million</output></label>
          <input id="copy-count" type="range" min="1" max="32" step="1" value="16"/>
          <div class="hardware-metrics"><div><span>Bytes read</span><strong data-copy-read>32 MB</strong></div><div><span>Bytes written</span><strong data-copy-write>32 MB</strong></div><div><span>Total traffic</span><strong data-copy-total>64 MB</strong></div><div><span>Time at 237.5 GB/s</span><strong data-copy-time>0.269 ms</strong></div></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation">Our RTX A3000 notebook measured about <strong>237.5 GB/s</strong> effective copy bandwidth. The slider scales the expected transfer time using that measured rate.</p>
          <p class="book-note">GB is decimal. Timing estimates assume the same sustained bandwidth; small copies can have additional overhead.</p>
          <span class="page-number">05</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-matrices" data-spread="matrices" aria-labelledby="title-matrices">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · COMPUTE</p>
          <h3 id="title-matrices">A matrix is a grid.<br/><em>A GPU does the arithmetic.</em></h3>
          <p>A <strong>vector</strong> is a line of numbers. A <strong>matrix</strong> is a grid. Neural networks use matrices to transform inputs into new representations.</p>
          <p>For each output number, multiply matching entries and add them together. The example on the right uses just two rows, but language models repeat this operation at enormous scale.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE VOCABULARY</span><p>A <strong>FLOP</strong> is one floating-point operation. <strong>FLOP/s</strong> is a rate. A trillion operations per second is one <strong>TFLOP/s</strong>. <strong>GEMV</strong> is matrix–vector multiplication; <strong>GEMM</strong> is matrix–matrix multiplication.</p></div>
          <p>GPU <strong>Tensor Cores</strong> are specialized execution units that accelerate supported matrix calculations.</p>
          <span class="page-number">06</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / MULTIPLY A ROW</span><span class="live-tag">STEP BY STEP</span></div>
          <div class="hardware-matrix-demo"><div class="hardware-grid" data-matrix-a><span>1</span><span>2</span><span>3</span><span>4</span><span>5</span><span>6</span></div><b>×</b><div class="hardware-grid vector"><span>2</span><span>1</span><span>4</span></div><b>=</b><div class="hardware-grid vector" data-matrix-result><span>?</span><span>?</span></div></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-matrix-explanation>Start with the first row. Each output is the sum of three multiplications.</p>
          <div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-matrix-next>Calculate next row →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-matrix-reset>Reset</button></div>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE LARGE VERSION</span><p>A 4096 × 4096 weight matrix multiplied by 512 input columns requires approximately <strong>17.18 billion operations</strong>. Our measured throughput for that shape was <strong>43.73 TFLOP/s</strong>.</p></div>
          <span class="page-number">07</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-reuse" data-spread="reuse" aria-labelledby="title-reuse">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · REUSE</p>
          <h3 id="title-reuse">Fetch once.<br/><em>Do more useful work.</em></h3>
          <p>Imagine opening a cookbook for one recipe. Now prepare many dishes while the same book is open. You get more use from the same retrieval.</p>
          <p>Similarly, a matrix multiplication can reuse the same <strong>weights</strong> across many input columns. This increases the opportunity to do more arithmetic per byte moved.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE KEY TERM</span><p><strong>Arithmetic intensity</strong> = operations performed ÷ bytes transferred. Higher intensity can move a workload away from a memory bottleneck.</p></div>
          <p>Batching changes the shape of the work, not just the number of tasks. More batching is not guaranteed to be faster because different kernels and memory behaviors matter.</p>
          <span class="page-number">08</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / REUSE THE WEIGHTS</span><span class="live-tag">RTX A3000 RESULTS</span></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="reuse-batch">Input columns <output id="reuse-batch-value">512</output></label>
          <input id="reuse-batch" type="range" min="0" max="6" step="1" value="3"/>
          <div class="hardware-metrics"><div><span>Arithmetic intensity</span><strong data-reuse-ai>409.6</strong></div><div><span>Achieved throughput</span><strong data-reuse-tf>43.73 TFLOP/s</strong></div></div>
          <div class="hardware-bars"><label>Work per byte</label><div><i data-reuse-ai-bar></i></div><label>Operations per second</label><div><i data-reuse-tf-bar></i></div></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-reuse-explanation>At 512 columns, the weights can be reused across many inputs. This workload achieved the highest throughput in our consolidated sweep.</p>
          <p class="book-note">The 1- and 32-column teaching points use estimated latencies because their original trials were inconsistent. Other points are measured. Arithmetic intensity uses idealized tensor traffic.</p>
          <span class="page-number">09</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-limits" data-spread="limits" aria-labelledby="title-limits">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · ROOFLINE</p>
          <h3 id="title-limits">Two speed limits.<br/><em>Only one binds first.</em></h3>
          <p>Picture the same factory. On some days, workers stand ready because materials have not arrived. On other days, deliveries pile up because the workers are fully occupied.</p>
          <p>The <strong>roofline model</strong> combines those two limits: <strong>memory bandwidth</strong> and <strong>compute throughput</strong>.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>WHY IT MATTERS</span><p>If workers wait for data, buying faster workers may not help. If workers are already busy, widening the loading dock may not help. First identify the bottleneck; then choose the optimization.</p></div>
          <p>This becomes crucial for LLMs: processing many prompt tokens can reuse weights, while generating one token often offers much less reuse.</p>
          <span class="page-number">10</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FIND THE BOTTLENECK</span><span class="live-tag">ROOFLINE</span></div>
          <svg class="hardware-roofline-plot" viewBox="0 0 450 245" role="img" aria-label="Roofline graph: sloping bandwidth limit and flat compute limit">
            <path d="M40 18 V207 H435" stroke="#8c9b8a" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/>
            <path d="M40 207 L260 55 H425" stroke="#214d3c" stroke-width="4" fill="none"/>
            <path d="M260 55 V207" stroke="#aab7a8" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
            <circle cx="260" cy="55" r="5" fill="#b77b48"/>
            <circle cx="145" cy="134" r="9" fill="#b77b48" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" data-roofline-cursor/>
            <text x="72" y="83" font-size="11" fill="#66776b">MEMORY LIMIT</text>
            <text x="290" y="41" font-size="11" fill="#66776b">COMPUTE LIMIT</text>
            <text x="244" y="222" font-size="10" fill="#66776b">RIDGE</text>
            <text x="225" y="241" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#66776b">Arithmetic intensity →</text>
          </svg>
          <label class="range-label" for="hardware-roof-slider">Work per byte <output id="hardware-roof-value">50 FLOPs/byte</output></label>
          <input id="hardware-roof-slider" type="range" min="1" max="400" step="1" value="50"/>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-hardware-roof-description>Workers wait for data. Reducing memory traffic or reusing weights could help.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>OUR MEASURED REFERENCE POINTS</span><p>Memory copy: <strong>237.5 GB/s</strong>. Best measured GEMM: <strong>43.73 TFLOP/s</strong>. Their simplified crossover is about <strong>184 FLOPs/byte</strong>.</p></div>
          <span class="page-number">11</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-evidence" data-spread="evidence" aria-labelledby="title-evidence">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · MEASUREMENT</p>
          <h3 id="title-evidence">Fast numbers are exciting.<br/><em>Trustworthy numbers are useful.</em></h3>
          <p>GPU work is often asynchronous: the CPU can queue a calculation before the GPU has finished it. That is why we measure elapsed device time using <strong>CUDA events</strong> and proper synchronization.</p>
          <p>We also <strong>warm up</strong> the GPU, repeat experiments, and inspect the variation. One unusually fast or slow number does not explain a system.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>OUR RULE</span><p>Keep measured results separate from estimates. An estimate can help explain a concept, but it cannot prove an optimization worked.</p></div>
          <p>We now have the vocabulary needed to follow a real model through memory, matrix calculations, and token generation.</p>
          <span class="page-number">12</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / PERFORMANCE DETECTIVE</span><span class="live-tag">BENCHMARK EVIDENCE</span></div>
          <p>One batch-32 run reported a median of <strong>2.7542 ms</strong> but a fastest trial of <strong>0.1564 ms</strong>. Which number proves steady-state GPU performance?</p>
          <div class="hardware-answer-options"><button data-evidence-answer="median">The median</button><button data-evidence-answer="fastest">The fastest</button><button data-evidence-answer="neither">Neither alone</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-evidence-feedback aria-live="polite">Choose an answer, then compare what each measurement can and cannot establish.</p>
          <div class="hardware-metrics"><div><span>Memory capacity</span><strong>6 GiB</strong></div><div><span>Copy bandwidth</span><strong>237.5 GB/s</strong></div><div><span>Best GEMM</span><strong>43.73 TFLOP/s</strong></div><div><span>SM count</span><strong>32</strong></div></div>
          <p class="book-note">These are notebook observations for the RTX A3000, not theoretical GPU peak specifications.</p>
          <span class="page-number">13</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-tokens" data-spread="tokens" aria-labelledby="title-tokens">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / THE GENERATION LOOP</p>
          <h3 id="title-tokens">A reply is built<br/><em>one token at a time.</em></h3>
          <p>First, the model processes the prompt. That is <strong>prefill</strong>. Then it generates a token, appends it to the context, and runs again. That is <strong>decode</strong>.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THINK OF IT THIS WAY</span><p>Read the question once. Write the answer a small piece at a time, checking what came before.</p></div>
          <p>Prompt tokens can be processed in parallel with a causal mask. Generated tokens depend on earlier choices, so their steps happen in sequence.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/transformer-forward-pass/`}>Go deeper: transformer inference mechanics →</a>
          <span class="page-number">02</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment" data-token-demo>
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FOLLOW THE TOKENS</span><span class="live-tag">STEP BY STEP</span></div>
          <p class="demo-caption">Illustrative tokens, not a tokenizer trace.</p>
          <div class="token-question"><span>THE PROMPT</span><div><b class="prompt-token">The</b><b class="prompt-token">sky</b><b class="prompt-token">is</b></div></div>
          <div class="model-box"><span>TRANSFORMER</span><div class="model-layers"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><small data-token-phase>Ready to read the prompt</small></div>
          <div class="token-answer"><span>THE CONTINUATION</span><div>{['blue', 'on', 'a', 'clear', 'day', '.'].map((t, i) => <b class="output-token" data-token={i} aria-hidden="true">{t}</b>)}</div></div>
          <div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-token-next>Run prefill →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-token-reset>Reset</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-token-explanation aria-live="polite">Start with prefill. The three prompt tokens pass through the model together.</p>
          <span class="page-number">03</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-cache" data-spread="cache" aria-labelledby="title-cache">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">02 / THE KV CACHE</p>
          <h3 id="title-cache">Keep the past.<br/><em>Compute the new.</em></h3>
          <p>Without a cache, a basic implementation recomputes old tokens’ keys and values at every generation step.</p>
          <p>A <strong>KV cache</strong> stores those keys and values. Each new token adds its own entry and attends to the stored context.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE TRADEOFF</span><p>Less repeated computation. More memory held for every active request.</p></div>
          <p>The cache does <em>not</em> remove attention to earlier tokens. Decode still reads the growing cache, which is why long contexts cost memory and bandwidth.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/kv-cache/`}>Go deeper: KV cache optimization →</a>
          <span class="page-number">04</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / REUSE THE PAST</span><span class="live-tag">SIMPLIFIED MODEL</span></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="cache-length">Prompt length <output id="cache-length-value">8 tokens</output></label>
          <input id="cache-length" type="range" min="4" max="24" step="4" value="8" />
          <div class="cache-legend"><span><i class="new-dot"></i> Recomputed / new</span><span><i class="saved-dot"></i> Reused from cache</span></div>
          <div class="cache-compare"><div><h4>Without cache</h4><div class="cache-grid" data-cache-uncached aria-hidden="true"></div><p><strong data-cache-uncached-count>30</strong> token positions projected</p></div><div><h4>With cache</h4><div class="cache-grid" data-cache-cached aria-hidden="true"></div><p><strong>3</strong> new token positions projected</p></div></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-cache-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="book-note">Three decode steps after prefill. Each square represents a token position’s K/V projection. This comparison excludes prefill and does not estimate total FLOPs or speedup.</p>
          <span class="page-number">05</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-roofline" data-spread="roofline" aria-labelledby="title-roofline">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">03 / THE ROOFLINE MODEL</p>
          <h3 id="title-roofline">Is the GPU waiting<br/><em>for the next byte?</em></h3>
          <p>A GPU can only calculate as fast as it can get its data. Two ceilings matter: <strong>memory bandwidth</strong> and <strong>compute throughput</strong>.</p>
          <p><strong>Arithmetic intensity</strong> means FLOPs performed per byte transferred. More reuse means more computation for each byte moved.</p>
          <div class="formula-card">Attainable TFLOP/s = min(<br/><span>peak TFLOP/s, FLOPs/byte × GB/s ÷ 1000</span>)</div>
          <p>Move the slider. Before the knee, bandwidth limits the ceiling. After it, compute does. Real kernels usually operate below this ideal roof.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/gpu-fundamentals/`}>Go deeper: GPU performance fundamentals →</a>
          <span class="page-number">06</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FIND THE LIMIT</span><span class="live-tag">ILLUSTRATIVE GPU</span></div>
          <div class="hardware-spec"><span><b>100</b> TFLOP/s peak</span><span><b>1,000</b> GB/s bandwidth</span></div>
          <svg class="roofline-chart" viewBox="0 0 450 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="roof-chart-title roof-chart-desc"><title id="roof-chart-title">Interactive roofline ceiling</title><desc id="roof-chart-desc">Linear axes. The bandwidth ceiling rises until 100 FLOPs per byte, where it meets the 100 TFLOP per second compute ceiling. The marker shows the selected ideal limit.</desc><g class="chart-grid"><path d="M55 50H425M55 125H425M55 200H425"/></g><path d="M55 25V205H425" class="chart-axis"/><path d="M55 200L203 50H425" class="roof-path"/><path d="M55 200L203 50" class="memory-path"/><text x="8" y="54">100</text><text x="15" y="129">50</text><text x="22" y="204">0</text><text x="48" y="224">0</text><text x="190" y="224">100</text><text x="337" y="224">200</text><text x="172" y="250">Arithmetic intensity (FLOPs/byte)</text><text x="57" y="17">Ideal ceiling (TFLOP/s)</text><circle data-roof-point cx="85" cy="170" r="7"/><path data-roof-guide d="M85 200V170H55" class="point-guide"/></svg>
          <label class="range-label" for="roof-intensity">Arithmetic intensity <output id="roof-intensity-value">20 FLOPs/byte</output></label>
          <input id="roof-intensity" type="range" min="5" max="250" step="5" value="20" />
          <div class="roof-result"><strong data-roof-bound>Memory-bound</strong><span><b data-roof-ceiling>20</b> TFLOP/s ideal limit</span></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-roof-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <span class="page-number">07</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-precision" data-spread="precision" aria-labelledby="title-precision">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">04 / WEIGHT PRECISION</p>
          <h3 id="title-precision">Smaller numbers.<br/><em>A lighter backpack.</em></h3>
          <p>A seven-billion-parameter model has seven billion stored weights. How many bits you use for each weight changes how much memory they occupy.</p>
          <div class="formula-card">Weight memory = parameters × bits ÷ 8</div>
          <p>Lower precision can make a model fit and reduce weight traffic. It can also change quality. Actual speed depends on supported kernels and the workload.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>CAPACITY IS NOT THE WHOLE STORY</span><p>You still need room for the KV cache, activations, workspace, and quantization metadata.</p></div>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/quantization/`}>Go deeper: quantization →</a>
          <span class="page-number">08</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / PACK THE WEIGHTS</span><span class="live-tag">7B PARAMETER MODEL</span></div>
          <fieldset class="precision-options"><legend>Choose a weight representation</legend>{[{bits:32,label:'FP32'},{bits:16,label:'FP16'},{bits:8,label:'INT8'},{bits:4,label:'INT4'}].map(p => <label><input type="radio" name="weight-precision" value={p.bits} checked={p.bits===16}/><span>{p.label}<small>{p.bits} bits</small></span></label>)}</fieldset>
          <div class="memory-tank"><div class="memory-fill" data-weight-fill></div><div><strong data-weight-gb>14</strong><span>GB / weights only</span></div></div>
          <div class="memory-scale"><span>0 GB</span><span>28 GB (FP32 baseline)</span></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-weight-explanation aria-live="polite">FP16 uses 14 GB for the raw weights of a 7B model.</p>
          <p class="book-note">Decimal GB (10⁹ bytes). Raw storage calculation, not a measured footprint or a promise that the model fits in a particular GPU.</p>
          <span class="page-number">09</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-batching" data-spread="batching" aria-labelledby="title-batching">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">05 / CONTINUOUS BATCHING</p>
          <h3 id="title-batching">An empty seat?<br/><em>Let the next request in.</em></h3>
          <p>Requests finish at different times. With a fixed batch, a short request leaves an empty slot while its longer neighbors keep running.</p>
          <p><strong>Continuous batching</strong> admits a waiting request at a step boundary as soon as a slot becomes free.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>WHAT THE ANIMATION SHOWS</span><p>Six requests. Three slots. The same amount of token work, with fewer empty slots between requests.</p></div>
          <p>Real serving systems also balance prefill, KV memory, fairness, and latency. This toy schedule isolates decode-slot reuse.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/continuous-batching/`}>Go deeper: serving systems architecture →</a>
          <span class="page-number">10</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / WATCH THE SCHEDULE</span><span class="live-tag">UNIT DECODE STEPS</span></div>
          <div class="batch-requests" aria-label="Requests and required decode steps">{[2,5,3,2,4,1].map((n,i)=><span class={`request-${i}`}>{String.fromCharCode(65+i)} <small>{n} {n === 1 ? 'step' : 'steps'}</small></span>)}</div>
          <div class="schedule-compare"><div><h4>Fixed batches <span data-fixed-count></span></h4><div class="schedule" data-fixed-schedule></div></div><div><h4>Continuous <span data-continuous-count></span></h4><div class="schedule" data-continuous-schedule></div></div></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="batch-step">Decode step <output id="batch-step-value">0 / 9</output></label>
          <input id="batch-step" type="range" min="0" max="9" step="1" value="0"/>
          <div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-batch-play>Play schedule →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-batch-reset>Reset</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-batch-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="book-note">All requests arrive at time zero; each generated token costs one equal step; prefill and scheduling overhead are excluded. Colors identify requests, not measured GPU utilization.</p>
          <span class="page-number">11</span>
        </div>
      </section>
